# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .gitattributes
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: every script in this repo is bash, and two of the three
# CI legs check the repo out on a machine where Git for Windows' default
# `core.autocrlf=true` is in effect (the `windows-2022` runner, and any
# contributor on Windows). Without this file `actions/checkout` rewrites
# scripts/*.sh with CRLF line endings and the MSYS2 leg dies immediately with
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
# or, once running, with
#     $'\r': command not found
# -- before a single line of FFmpeg is compiled, and with a diagnostic that
# reads like a corrupted toolchain rather than a checkout setting.
#
# The same hazard applies to versions.lock (a trailing \r would end up inside
# every pinned value, so `curl "$FFMPEG_URL"` would fetch a URL with a CR in
# it) and to flags/*.flags (a trailing \r would be appended to every configure
# flag). So the rule is repo-wide, not just for *.sh.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Normalise everything Git considers text to LF in the working tree, on every
# platform. This is the whole fix; the lines below only make the intent
# explicit for the files where a CRLF would be fatal rather than merely ugly.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh          text eol=lf
*.flags       text eol=lf
*.lock        text eol=lf
*.md          text eol=lf
*.yml         text eol=lf
*.yaml        text eol=lf
*.txt         text eol=lf
*.in          text eol=lf
Dockerfile*   text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore    text eol=lf
LICENSE       text eol=lf

# Never touch binaries: a fetched tarball or an archive under dist/ must stay
# byte-identical to what we hashed.
*.tar.xz  binary
*.tar.gz  binary
*.zip     binary
*.xz      binary
*.gz      binary
*.png     binary
*.asc     binary
